BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Doctoral Colloquium (Music) | Laura Risk
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T180000
DTSTAMP:20261010T022401Z
UID:mcgill-arts-374788-0@mcgill-calendar.invalid
SEQUENCE:0
DESCRIPTION:Doctoral Colloquium: Dr Laura Risk\, Associate Professor of M
 usic\, University of Toronto Scarborough\n\nTitle: “Love and wariness
 ”: Staged Veillées and the Performance of Traditional Music in Quebec\n
 \nQuebec has a complicated relationship with its traditional music. Even a
 s fiddles and accordions share festival stages with “electro-trad” ban
 ds\, traditional music and dance are often disparaged in the media as embl
 ematic of an outmoded and conservative past from which the province disass
 ociated itself with the Quiet Revolution. In this paper\, I posit that a p
 resent-day ambivalence towards traditional music—what one listener descr
 ibed as a combination of “love and wariness”—is grounded in the long
  history of what I term “staged veillées.” These commercial performan
 ces of idealized rural life were the primary format for the presentation o
 f traditional music in Quebec for much of the twentieth century. This pape
 r analyzes the last long-running series of staged veillées\, the popular 
 television program Soirée canadienne (1960-1983)\, and its continued use 
 today as a metonym for kitsch in francophone media coverage of traditional
  music.\n\nLaura Risk is an Associate Professor of Music at the University
  of Toronto Scarborough\, with a cross-appointment to the Faculty of Music
  at the University of Toronto. She is the author of Music and the Staged V
 eillée in Quebec: Performing Tradition (University of Illinois Press\, 20
 26). Her album Traverse was awarded the 2024 Prix Opus for Album of the Ye
 ar in the category Traditional Québécois Music.\n\nThe Doctoral Colloqui
 um is open to all.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261010T022401Z
LOCATION:A-832\, Elizabeth Wirth Music Building\, CA\, QC\, Montreal\, H3A
  1E3\, 527 rue Sherbrooke Ouest
URL:https://www.mcgill.ca/channels/channels/event/doctoral-colloquium-musi
 c-laura-risk-374788
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Doctoral Colloquium (Music) | Caroline Holden
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T180000
DTSTAMP:20261010T022401Z
UID:mcgill-arts-374789-0@mcgill-calendar.invalid
SEQUENCE:0
DESCRIPTION:Doctoral Colloquium: Caroline Holden\, Ph.D. candidate at McG
 ill\n\nTitle: Rumination in University-Level Music Students: The Costs of
  Repetitive Negative Thinking\n\nRumination—the tendency to become caugh
 t in repetitive\, negative thinking—has been associated with a wide rang
 e of psychological difficulties\, yet it has received relatively little at
 tention in the context of advanced music training. University music studen
 ts regularly encounter high expectations\, continual evaluation\, competit
 ion\, and frequent comparison with peers\, conditions that may make repeti
 tive self-evaluative thinking particularly consequential. Drawing on quant
 itative and qualitative data from university-level music students\, this r
 esearch examines the role of rumination in their experiences of musical tr
 aining. Quantitative findings explore the relationships among rumination\,
  perfectionism\, and music performance anxiety\, while qualitative account
 s examine how rumination emerges\, persists\, and shapes students’ respo
 nses to mistakes\, criticism\, evaluation\, and self-doubt. Together\, the
  findings position rumination as an important psychological process throug
 h which experiences within advanced music training may continue beyond the
  immediate situation\, influencing students’ anxiety\, confidence\, prac
 tice\, and well-being.\n\nCaroline Holden is a PhD candidate in Applied Pe
 rformance Sciences at McGill University. A classically trained singer\, pe
 rformer\, and voice teacher\, her research draws on her background in musi
 c performance and psychology to examine rumination and the psychological e
 xperiences of university-level music students.\n\nThe Doctoral Colloquium 
 is open to all.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261010T022401Z
LOCATION:A-832\, Elizabeth Wirth Music Building\, CA\, QC\, Montreal\, H3A
  1E3\, 527 rue Sherbrooke Ouest
URL:https://www.mcgill.ca/channels/channels/event/doctoral-colloquium-musi
 c-caroline-holden-374789
END:VEVENT
